===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q659}}The direction towards the [[pole]] to the left-hand side of someone facing [[east]], specifically 0°, or (on another celestial object) the direction towards the [[pole]] lying on the northern side of the [[invariable plane]].
#: {{alti|en|N|q=abbreviation}}
#: {{ux|en|Minnesota is in the '''north''' of the USA.}}
# The [[up]] or [[positive]] direction.
#: {{ux|en|Stock prices are heading back towards the '''north'''.}}
# {{lb|en|physics}} The [[positive]] or [[north pole]] of a [[magnet]], which seeks the magnetic pole near Earth's geographic [[North Pole]] (which, for its magnetic properties, is a south pole).
# {{alt case|en|North||a northern region; the inhabitants thereof}}. {{defdate|circa 1300}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Mats Lundahl|title=Politics or Markets?: Essays on Haitian Underdevelopment|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781134950997
|passage={{...}} and after independence the '''north''' clung to sugar production longer than the south, with the result that when the '''north''' took {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} In a church: the direction to the left-hand side of a person facing the [[altar]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Leonel L. Mitchell|title=Pastoral and Occasional Liturgies: A Ceremonial Guide|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield|isbn=9781561011582|page=49
|passage=If candidates stand on the liturgical south facing the presider and liturgical assistants on the liturgical '''north''', it will present better visual lines for the congregation than if they stand facing east and west with their backs toward the congregation.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Paul Turner|title=At the Supper of the Lamb: A Pastoral and Theological Commentary on the Mass|publisher=LiturgyTrainingPublications|isbn=9781568549217|page=27
|passage=Many early Christian basilicas were designed with twin ambos for the proclamation of the epistle (on the liturgical south side) and the Gospel (on the '''north'''). The separation of the ambos indicated the distinction that should be accorded the Gospel, which was proclaimed from the '''north''' as if evangelization needed to happen to the geographically southern part of the world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Paul Porwoll|title=Against All Odds: History of Saint Andrew's Parish Church, Charleston, 1706-2013|publisher=WestBow Press|isbn=9781490818177|page=365
|passage=At St. Andrew&#39;s, ecclesiastical '''north''', south, east, and west correspond to geographical northeast, southwest, southeast, and northwest.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017|author=Cameron Macdonell|title=Ghost Storeys: Ralph Adams Cram, Modern Gothic Media, and Deconstructive Microhistory at a Canadian Church|publisher=McGill-Queen's Press - MQUP|isbn=9780773549913
|passage=The new St Mary's Anglican Church, Walkerville, has an attached rectory flanking to the liturgical south and an attached parish hall flanking to the liturgical '''north''', both half-timbered in the Tudor Revival style. [Referring to a church that is oriented SSE, making "south" WSW]<!-- See https://www.google.com/maps/place/St.+Mary's+Anglican+Church/@42.3199301,-83.0102212,103m/data=!3m1!1e3!4m5!3m4!1s0x883b2cbe430746a5:0x39cf8de2310f9988!8m2!3d42.3199811!4d-83.0100583 -->}}

